@page "/"
@implements IAsyncDisposable
@inject BenchClient Bench
@inject IJSRuntime JS
@inject NavigationManager Nav

@* The desktop app's main window, in the same three bands and the same order: the scan
   controls, the grid of what answered, and then the consoles — one tab per connected
   instrument, inside the group at the foot of the window.

   The tabs were at the top of the page with a Bench tab beside them, which made the bench
   itself one console among several and hid it whenever you were talking to an instrument.
   The desktop has never done that: the scan and the results are always on screen and the
   tabs live down in the Instrument Consoles box, so you can watch a sweep find something and
   drive what it found without either one going away.

   Every console stays in the render tree, hidden rather than removed, so switching tabs
   keeps each one's log, readings and half-typed command. That is the whole point of tabs.
   A console in its own browser window is the exception: it is genuinely elsewhere, so its
   tab dims and its pane says so. *@

@if (_blocked is not null)
{
    <p class="error" style="margin:-.4rem 0 .85rem">
        The browser blocked that window. Allow pop-ups for this site, or
        <a href="@_blocked" target="@_blockedName">open the console in a tab from here</a>.
    </p>
}

@* ------------------------------------------------------------------- the bench *@

<div class="group">
    @* The captions and the field labels are the desktop's, letter for letter, down to the
       colons and the capitals. Someone who has used the app on the bench PC is reading the
       same words in the same order; sentence case here and title case there is two apps. *@
    @* The heading, made a control — the desktop's, and the same switch it draws there.

       Each half is a whole caption rather than a word with a shared "Scan" after it: the two
       read as the two things this card can be, and the selected half is the card's title
       outright.

       It is the heading of this card because that is the honest scope of it: the choice
       governs the inputs here, the columns of the list below, and which box the Address row
       shows. A switch on the address row instead left this card still saying "Network Scan"
       while the box under it held COM3.

       Network is the default in both builds and is not remembered: opening onto a card
       listing the ports of a machine whose adapter may not be plugged in costs far more than
       the one click it saves. *@
    <span class="cap">
        <span class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Which kind of bench">
            <button class="@(_serial ? "" : "on")" @onclick="() => SetScanKind(false)" disabled="@_scanning"
                    title="Look for instruments over the network — a sweep of the subnet or the IP range given. This is the default.">Network Scan</button>
            <button class="@(_serial ? "on" : "")" @onclick="() => SetScanKind(true)" disabled="@_scanning"
                    title="Look at the server's RS-232 serial ports instead, including any USB-to-serial adapter. They are listed rather than swept: a serial port cannot be discovered the way a subnet can, so Scan asks the ports the server already has.">Serial Scan</button>
        </span>
    </span>

    @* One row, and only the half of it that belongs to the chosen bench. The button that
       answers the row is outside the branch: there is one Scan, and which sweep it starts is
       the switch above, not a second control. *@
    <div class="row">
        @if (!_serial)
        {
        <label for="iface" title="Which of this PC's network adapters to search on.">Interface:</label>
        <select id="iface" @bind="_interfaceAddress" @bind:after="InterfaceChanged" disabled="@_scanning"
                title="Network adapter and subnet to scan. Pick the one on the same network as your instruments — the host count shows how many addresses will be probed.">
            @foreach (var i in _interfaces)
            {
                <option value="@i.Address">@i.Address/@i.PrefixLength — @i.Name (@i.HostCount hosts)</option>
            }
        </select>

        <label for="range" title="Which addresses to probe. Empty scans the whole subnet.">IP range:</label>
        <input id="range" type="text" @bind="_range" size="20" disabled="@_scanning"
               placeholder="blank sweeps the whole subnet" title="@RangeHelp" />

        @* Filled, not hinted. The desktop's is a drop-down with these seven presets and the
           last of them already in the box; here that is an input with a datalist, which is
           the same control. The placeholder it replaces said "5025,111" while an empty box
           actually swept all four of NetworkScanner.CommonScpiPorts — a hint that was not
           only unfilled but wrong. *@
        <label for="ports" title="TCP ports probed on each address.">SCPI Port(s):</label>
        <input id="ports" type="text" @bind="_ports" list="port-presets" size="20" disabled="@_scanning"
               title="TCP ports to probe, comma-separated. 5025 is the usual raw SCPI socket, 5555 is Rigol's, 111 is the RPC portmapper used to find VXI-11 instruments, and 3490 is where a Fluke 8845A/8846A answers." />
        <datalist id="port-presets">
            <option value="5025"></option>
            <option value="5555"></option>
            <option value="3490"></option>
            <option value="111"></option>
            <option value="5025, 5555"></option>
            <option value="5025, 5555, 111"></option>
            <option value="5025, 5555, 3490, 111"></option>
        </datalist>
        }
        else
        {
        @* The same two questions asked of a serial bench: which ports, and at what speed.
           "Ports:" is the counterpart of Interface — how much of the bench this covers, and
           how much of it there is — and the baud list is the counterpart of SCPI Port(s):
           a short written list of the values worth trying, tried in order. *@
        <label for="scope" title="Which of the server's serial ports Scan opens.">Ports:</label>
        <select id="scope" @bind="_serialScope" disabled="@_scanning"
                title="All of the server's serial ports, or a single one. Re-read each time this card switches to Serial, so an adapter plugged in just now is in the list.">
            <option value="">All serial ports — @_serialPorts.Count found</option>
            @foreach (string p in _serialPorts)
            {
                <option value="@p">@p</option>
            }
        </select>

        <label for="bauds" title="The baud rates Scan tries on each port.">Baud rate(s):</label>
        <input id="bauds" type="text" @bind="_bauds" list="baud-presets" size="26" disabled="@_scanning"
               title="Tried in the order written, stopping at the first that answers. Most instruments ship at 9600; anything made this century is usually set to 115200. Everything else stays at 8-N-1 with no flow control — set those per port in the Address box if one needs them." />
        <datalist id="baud-presets">
            <option value="9600"></option>
            <option value="115200"></option>
            <option value="9600, 115200"></option>
            <option value="9600, 115200, 19200, 38400, 57600"></option>
            <option value="1200"></option>
            <option value="2400"></option>
            <option value="4800"></option>
            <option value="19200"></option>
            <option value="38400"></option>
            <option value="57600"></option>
        </datalist>
        }

        @* The same wide step as before Connect below, and for the same reason: the inputs are
           one question, and Scan is what answers it. *@
        <span class="answer"></span>

        @* One button, two jobs — the desktop's Scan does the same. A sweep of a /20 runs
           for minutes, and the thing you want while it does is the way out of it. *@
        @if (_scanning)
        {
            <button class="danger" @onclick="Stop" title="Stop the running scan. Whatever has answered so far stays in the list."><Icon Name="stop" /> Stop</button>
        }
        else
        {
            <button class="primary" @onclick="Scan"
                    title="@(_serial ? ScanSerialHelp : ScanNetworkHelp)"><Icon Name="scan" /> Scan</button>
        }
    </div>

    @* Always here, with nothing running. This row is the full width of the card and the
       list of found instruments sits directly under it, so a row that appears when Scan is
       pressed shoves that list down at the exact moment you have started watching it — and
       shoves it again when the bar goes away at the end. The desktop's scan group keeps
       its bar and its "Ready." on screen for the same reason.

       Determinate: the address list is counted before the first probe, so the bar can say
       how far along it is rather than only that it is busy. Counted in per cent rather
       than in hosts, so the scale still means something before there is a host count. *@
    <div class="row" style="margin-top:.55rem">
        <div class="bar" role="progressbar" aria-label="Scan progress"
             title="How far the current scan has progressed through the subnet."
             aria-valuemin="0" aria-valuemax="100" aria-valuenow="@Percent">
            <div class="fill" style="width:@(Percent)%"></div>
        </div>
        @* Clipped to one line by the stylesheet; the whole of it stays in the tooltip. *@
        <span class="muted" title="@_scanStatus">@_scanStatus</span>
    </div>

    @if (!_serial && _interfaces.Count == 0)
    {
        <p class="error" style="margin:.6rem 0 0">
            The server can see no usable network interface. In Docker that almost always means the
            container is on a bridge network instead of the host's — see the compose file.
        </p>
    }
    @* The same sentence for the same shape of problem, and the same fix in the same file: a
       container starts with no serial ports, and host networking does not carry them. *@
    @if (_serial && _serialPorts.Count == 0)
    {
        <p class="error" style="margin:.6rem 0 0">
            The server has no serial ports. In Docker they have to be passed in one at a time
            with a <code>devices:</code> entry — see the compose file.
        </p>
    }
</div>

<div class="group">
    <span class="cap">
        Discovered Instruments
        @* Always, as the desktop's group box says it always. It described a gesture that only
           existed once there was a row to make it on, which is the wrong way round: the thing
           you need to be told before you have tried anything is how to use what appears. *@
        <span class="hint">(double-click a row to connect)</span>
    </span>

    @* The table stands whether or not anything has answered, as the desktop's list view does:
       a ListView with columns draws its header row from the moment the window opens. A
       sentence in its place said what the empty table says by being empty, and moved the
       four headings down the card every time a scan found its first instrument. *@
    @* The list and the row that acts on it are one block, so the table is never narrower than
       the controls under it: its right edge lands on Connect's rather than stopping short in
       the middle of the card, which read as a table that had failed to draw. The wider of the
       two sets the width — a scan that finds a long identity string widens the frame, and the
       row stays where it is. *@
    <div class="fitcol">
    <div class="scroll fit" style="max-height:260px; margin-bottom:.6rem"
         title="Instruments found by the last scan. Click a row to put its address in the box below; double-click to open a console for it.">
        <table>
            <thead>
                @* The desktop's four column headings, letter for letter — they are also
                   the header row Core writes into the exported CSV, so the screen and the
                   file name the same four things the same way. *@
                @* The first two hold different things in the two modes and are named for
                   what they hold: an address and a TCP port, or a port name and its line
                   settings. The other two are the same either way. *@
                <tr>
                    <th>@(_serial ? "Port" : "IP Address")</th>
                    <th>@(_serial ? "Settings" : "Port")</th>
                    <th>Protocol</th>
                    @* Export Results lives in the last heading, hard against its right edge —
                       the corner of the box it writes out, which is where the desktop pins it:
                       Place(btnExport, lstDevices.Right - btnExport.Width). Under the table it
                       needed a row of its own, and that row was one button and a card's width
                       of nothing. Dead until there is a list to write. *@
                    <th class="act">
                        <span class="cell">
                            Identity (*IDN?)
                            <button @onclick="ExportResults" disabled="@(_devices.Count == 0)"
                                    title="Save the discovered-instrument list to a CSV file.">
                                <Icon Name="savefile" /> Export Results&hellip;
                            </button>
                        </span>
                    </th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                @if (_devices.Count == 0)
                {
                    @* One empty row, always. A list view keeps its row area whether or not
                       anything is in it, and four headings standing alone on a card read as
                       a table that failed to draw rather than as one waiting to be filled.
                       The space is what carries the height; the other three cells are as
                       empty as they look. *@
                    <tr class="blank"><td class="mono">&nbsp;</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
                }
                @foreach (var d in _devices)
                {
                    @* Click selects and fills the Address box with the full endpoint —
                       including the port the scan actually found, which is the whole
                       point: a Rigol answering on 5555 is not the same address as one
                       assumed on 5025. Double-click connects, as on the desktop. *@
                    @* Highlighted when the box below holds this row, in either mode: the
                       serial box holds a port name and TypedAddress is what that becomes. *@
                    <tr class="clickable @(Endpoint(d) == TypedAddress ? "on" : "")"
                        @onclick="() => Pick(d)"
                        @ondblclick="() => Connect(Endpoint(d))">
                        <td class="mono">@d.Address</td>
                        @* A serial port has no port number; the second column carries the
                           line settings instead, which is what has to match before a
                           character gets through. *@
                        <td class="mono">@(_serial ? d.Settings : d.Port.ToString())</td>
                        <td>@d.Transport</td>
                        <td class="mono">@d.Identity</td>
                    </tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="row">
        <label for="addr" title="Address of the instrument to connect to.">Address:</label>

        @* Which of the two boxes shows is the scan card's switch, not a second switch here.
           One choice, one control: the card, the columns and this box are all about the same
           bench, and two switches that had to agree would eventually not. *@

        @* An address and nothing more. Stretched across the card it was a box the width of a
           paragraph waiting for twenty-one characters. Sized for the longest thing that goes
           in it — 255.255.255.255:65535 — and no further. *@
        @if (!_serial)
        {
            <input id="addr" class="mono" type="text" @bind="_address" size="22"
                   placeholder="192.168.1.50:5555"
                   title="Instrument address. Accepts a plain IP, an IP with port (192.168.1.50:5555), a vxi:// address, a serial port on the server (serial://COM3, or serial://COM3?baud=115200 at other line settings), or a VISA resource string (TCPIP0::192.168.1.50::inst0::INSTR)." />
        }
        else
        {
            @* The desktop's editable combo box, said the way a browser says it: a text input
               with a list attached, so a port can be chosen or typed. A plain select could
               only be chosen from, and the line settings have to be typeable. *@
            <input id="addr" class="mono" type="text" @bind="_serialPort" size="22"
                   list="serialports" @onfocus="LoadPortsAsync"
                   placeholder="@(_serialPorts.Count == 0 ? "no ports on the server" : "COM3")"
                   title="The serial ports on the machine running the server — a browser has none of its own. Type instead of choosing to reach a port that is not listed, or to set the line settings: COM3?baud=115200, and likewise parity, databits, stopbits, flow and term. Without them it is 9600-8-N-1, no flow control." />
            <datalist id="serialports">
                @foreach (string p in _serialPorts)
                {
                    <option value="@p"></option>
                }
            </datalist>
        }

        @* Beside Connect rather than up with the scan, because it is the instrument timeout:
           used when connecting and on every query afterwards. The scan has its own short,
           fixed probe timeouts, so a long value here never slows discovery. The desktop's
           numbers exactly — 100 to 30000, in steps of 500, starting at 3000. *@
        <label for="timeout" title="How long to wait for the instrument, when connecting and on every query afterwards.">Timeout (ms):</label>
        <input id="timeout" class="num" type="number" @bind="_timeoutMs" min="100" max="30000" step="500"
               title="Milliseconds to wait for the instrument. Applies to the connection and to every query on it afterwards, not to the scan — that has its own short probe timeouts." />

        @* The wide step the desktop puts before the control that answers a row: the address
           and the timeout are halves of one question and Connect is the answer to it, so it
           stands apart from them rather than at the end of a run of boxes. *@
        <span class="answer"></span>

        @* One button, two jobs, exactly as Scan does. Worth having because connecting is the
           one operation here that can hang for a long time with nothing to show for it: a
           raw-socket connect to an address that answers ARP but has nothing listening sits in
           SYN retransmits, and VXI-11 makes two of them. *@
        @if (_connecting)
        {
            <button class="danger" @onclick="CancelConnect"
                    title="Stop trying to connect. The attempt is dropped at the server, not merely ignored here.">
                <Icon Name="stop" /> Cancel
            </button>
        }
        else
        {
            <button @onclick="() => Connect(TypedAddress)"
                    title="Open a console for the address shown. Each instrument gets its own tab below, so several can be connected at once.">
                <Icon Name="connect" /> Connect
            </button>
        }
    </div>
    </div>

    @* Under the block rather than at the end of the row. A failed connect says why in a
       sentence — "no route to host", a VISA resource that parsed but answered nothing — and a
       sentence at the end of a row of boxes would decide how wide the block is. *@
    @if (_connectError is not null)
    {
        <p class="error" style="margin:.5rem 0 0">@_connectError</p>
    }
</div>

@* --------------------------------------------------------------- the consoles *@

@* The desktop's Instrument Consoles group: a tab per open instrument, the console of
   whichever tab is on, and — while nothing is connected — a note in place of the empty tab
   strip, because a blank panel with no explanation reads as a broken window. *@

<div class="group consoles">
    <div class="cap-row">
        <span class="cap">Instrument Consoles</span>
        <span class="spacer"></span>
        @* Always here, connected or not — as the desktop keeps its own, and for the same
           reason: a multi-instrument script is written before the instruments are connected
           as often as after. It used to sit at the end of the tab strip, which meant it did
           not exist until something was in that strip. *@
        <button @onclick="OpenSequences"
                title="Write and run one script that drives several instruments at once, for measurements where they have to take turns.">
            <Icon Name="script" /> Multi-Instrument Scripts&hellip;
        </button>
    </div>

    @if (_sessions.Count == 0)
    {
        @* "Scan the network above" is the one sentence on screen telling a new reader what to
           do next, so it has to be about the card that is actually above it. *@
        <p class="none">
            @if (_serial)
            {
                @:No instrument connected. Pick a serial port above and press Connect — or click
                @:one of the rows — to open a console for it. Each instrument gets its own tab,
                @:and any console can be opened in a browser tab of its own.
            }
            else
            {
                @:No instrument connected. Scan the network above, then click one of the results — or
                @:type its address and press Connect — to open a console for it. Each instrument gets
                @:its own tab, and any console can be opened in a browser tab of its own.
            }
        </p>
    }
    else
    {
        <div class="tabstrip" title="One tab per connected instrument. Click its ✕ to disconnect and close it. Right-click a tab to open that console in a browser tab of its own, or to disconnect it.">
            @foreach (var s in _sessions)
            {
                @* Right-click gives the same two things the desktop's tab menu gives. It is
                   the only place either of them is reachable without first bringing the tab
                   to the front, which is the point of a tab menu. *@
                <div class="tab @(s.Id == _selected ? "on" : "") @(IsOut(s.Id) ? "out" : "")" title="@s.Identity"
                     @oncontextmenu="e => OpenTabMenu(s.Id, e)" @oncontextmenu:preventDefault="true">
                    <button class="pick" @onclick="() => Show(s.Id)">
                        <span class="dot"></span>
                        <span>@s.ProfileName</span>
                        <span class="muted">@s.Address</span>
                    </button>
                    @* On the tab rather than in the console, because it is a thing you do *to* a
                       tab — the same place a browser puts the ✕ beside it. It was a labelled
                       button on the console's identity line, which cost a row's width to say what
                       the tab can say with a glyph, and could only be reached by first bringing
                       the console to the front.

                       A link, not a button calling window.open: it is an address, so nothing can
                       pop-up-block it and the browser's own conventions come free — Shift for a
                       separate window, Ctrl for a background tab, middle-click for a tab. The
                       target names the tab after the session, so pressing it twice comes back to
                       the console already open instead of starting a second. *@
                    <a class="pop" href="console?session=@s.Id&detached=1" target="lec-console-@s.Id"
                       title="Open this console in a browser tab of its own. Shift-click for a separate window."
                       @onclick:stopPropagation="true"><Icon Name="detach" /></a>
                    <button class="shut" title="Disconnect @s.Address and close this tab"
                            @onclick="() => Disconnect(s.Id)"><Icon Name="close" /></button>
                </div>
            }
        </div>

        @if (_tabMenu is not null)
        {
            <div class="scrim" @onmousedown="() => _downOutside = true" @onmouseup="ReleasedOutside"></div>
            <div class="menu tabmenu" role="group" aria-label="Tab actions"
                 style="left:@(_menuX.ToString(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture))px; top:@(_menuY.ToString(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture))px"
                 @onmousedown="() => _downOutside = false" @onmouseup="() => _downOutside = false">
                <a class="item" href="console?session=@_tabMenu&detached=1" target="lec-console-@_tabMenu"
                   @onclick="CloseTabMenu">
                    <Icon Name="detach" /> Open in new tab
                </a>
                <button class="item" @onclick="DisconnectFromMenu">
                    <Icon Name="close" /> Disconnect and close tab
                </button>
            </div>
        }

        @foreach (var s in _sessions)
        {
            if (IsOut(s.Id))
            {
                <div class="pane" hidden="@(_selected != s.Id)">
                    <p class="muted" style="margin:.7rem 0">
                        This console is open in another browser tab or window. The connection is
                        unaffected — it is the same instrument and the same session, being driven
                        from over there.
                    </p>
                    <div class="row">
                        @* Not a link: a link would navigate that window to this address again and
                           reload a console with a session's worth of log in it. This asks the
                           browser for the window by name and does nothing else to it. *@
                        <button @onclick="() => Raise(s.Id)"
                            title="Bring the browser window this console is in to the front, without reloading it."><Icon Name="detach" /> Show that window</button>
                        <button class="primary" @onclick="() => Recall(s.Id)"
                            title="Close that window and put this console back in its tab here."><Icon Name="attach" /> Bring it back here</button>
                    </div>
                </div>
            }
            else
            {
                <ConsoleView @key="s.Id" @ref="_consoles[s.Id]" Session="s"
                             Visible="@(_selected == s.Id)"
                             OnOpenTool="t => OpenTool(s.Id, t)" />
            }
        }
    }
</div>

@* The tool windows, outside the card the consoles are in.

   They belong to an instrument but not to its tab: a console that is not on top is hidden, and
   a window inside a hidden subtree is one nobody can see and nobody can dismiss - so a window
   built in there had to be closed on every tab switch. Out here they behave like the desktop’s,
   which is what they are: look at another instrument for a moment and the script editor you were
   writing in is still open, still on this one, and still says so in its title bar. *@
@foreach (var s in _sessions)
{
    if (!IsOut(s.Id))
    {
        <ConsoleTools @key="s.Id" @ref="_tools[s.Id]" Session="s"
                      OnInsert="c => Insert(s.Id, c)" />
    }
}

<WindowDialog @ref="_windows" />

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Which console to open on arrival. It is how /console?session=… hands over: an attached
    /// console lives here, so that address redirects to this one rather than growing a second
    /// home for the same thing.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter, SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = "session")]
    public string? SessionFromQuery { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The script editor, opened over the bench rather than instead of it.</summary>
    private WindowDialog? _windows;

    /// <summary>
    /// Every open console and its windows, by session, so a button in one can reach the other.
    /// They are in different parts of the tree on purpose - see the tool windows above - which
    /// leaves the page to carry the two things that cross between them: which tool was asked for,
    /// and which command the reference handed back.
    /// </summary>
    private readonly Dictionary<string, ConsoleView> _consoles = [];
    private readonly Dictionary<string, ConsoleTools> _tools = [];

    /// <summary>A tool button in one of the consoles.</summary>
    private async Task OpenTool(string id, ConsoleTool tool)
    {
        if (_tools.TryGetValue(id, out var windows)) await windows.OpenAsync(tool);
    }

    /// <summary>A command chosen in that instrument’s Command Reference.</summary>
    private void Insert(string id, string command) => ConsoleFor(id)?.Insert(command);

    /// <summary>
    /// The console showing this session right now, if one is - named after the desktop’s own
    /// ConsoleFor. A session that has been disconnected, or whose console is open in a browser
    /// tab of its own, has none here, and the captured reference to the last one is stale.
    /// </summary>
    private ConsoleView? ConsoleFor(string id) =>
        !IsOut(id) && _sessions.Any(s => s.Id == id) && _consoles.TryGetValue(id, out var console)
            ? console
            : null;

    private async Task OpenSequences()
    {
        if (_windows is not null) await _windows.ShowSequencesAsync();
    }

    private IReadOnlyList<LocalInterfaceDto> _interfaces = [];
    private IReadOnlyList<SessionDto> _sessions = [];

    /// <summary>What has answered so far. Appended to during a sweep, not replaced at the
    /// end of one — an instrument is listed the moment it answers.</summary>
    private readonly List<DeviceDto> _devices = [];
    private string? _interfaceAddress;
    /// <summary>
    /// What the range box accepts, spelled out. The desktop's tooltip for this field is the
    /// only documentation any of the forms have, and it is the field people get wrong.
    /// </summary>
    private const string RangeHelp =
        "Narrow the scan to part of the subnet. Leave it empty to sweep all of it.\n\n"
      + "192.168.1.20-60      a range\n"
      + "20-60                the same, in the selected adapter's subnet\n"
      + "192.168.1.50         one address\n"
      + "192.168.1.0/28       a block\n"
      + "20-40, 55, 90-99     any mixture, comma-separated\n\n"
      + "Worth using on a shared lab network: a full sweep knocks on every printer, camera "
      + "and PLC as well as your instruments.";

    /// <summary>
    /// What to sweep, filled in with the chosen adapter's subnet rather than hinted at with an
    /// example of one. Blank still means the whole subnet — it is the same set of addresses —
    /// but a box that says 192.168.1.0/24 is a box you can read, and the one that said
    /// "20-60, or blank for all" was an example of a thing you might type instead.
    /// </summary>
    private string _range = "";

    /// <summary>
    /// Follow the adapter, but only over a range nobody has typed. Anything still equal to
    /// some adapter's subnet counts as untouched — the same rule the AI box uses for the
    /// endpoint and model it fills in.
    /// </summary>
    private void InterfaceChanged()
    {
        string? whole = _interfaces.FirstOrDefault(i => i.Address == _interfaceAddress)?.WholeRange;
        if (whole is null) return;

        bool untouched = _range.Trim().Length == 0
                      || _interfaces.Any(i => i.WholeRange == _range.Trim());
        if (untouched) _range = whole;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The four ports a scan sweeps when nobody says otherwise — NetworkScanner.CommonScpiPorts,
    /// written out, which is what the desktop's port box starts with too. In the box rather
    /// than behind it: an empty field that quietly means "all four" is a field you cannot read.
    /// </summary>
    private string _ports = "5025, 5555, 3490, 111";
    private string _address = "";

    /// <summary>
    /// The whole card is on Serial rather than Network: the scan inputs, the columns of the
    /// list, and which box the Address row shows. Not remembered between visits, for the
    /// reason the desktop does not remember it either: a page that opens on a serial card is
    /// a page listing the ports of a server whose adapter may not be plugged in this morning.
    /// </summary>
    private bool _serial;

    /// <summary>What is in the serial box — a port name, and optionally its line settings.</summary>
    private string _serialPort = "";

    /// <summary>
    /// Which ports Scan opens: one by name, or empty for all of them. The counterpart of the
    /// IP range rather than of the interface — how much of the bench to cover.
    /// </summary>
    private string _serialScope = "";

    /// <summary>
    /// The rates Scan tries on each port, in order, stopping at the first that answers. The
    /// counterpart of SCPI Port(s), and filled rather than hinted for the same reason that
    /// box is: an empty box would still sweep the defaults, and a hint that does not say
    /// which ones is a hint that is wrong.
    /// </summary>
    private string _bauds = "9600, 115200, 19200, 38400, 57600";

    private const string ScanNetworkHelp =
        "Search for instruments — the whole subnet, or just the IP range if one is given. "
        + "Found devices appear in the list as they answer.";

    private const string ScanSerialHelp =
        "Open each listed port on the server and ask it for its identity, trying the baud "
        + "rates above in order. Every port stays listed either way; the ones that answer "
        + "gain an identity. This writes *IDN? to whatever is on the other end, and opening "
        + "a port asserts DTR and RTS — which resets some boards.";

    /// <summary>
    /// The server's serial ports, for the list attached to that box. The server's, because a
    /// browser has none: every instrument operation here is the server's, and this is the one
    /// where saying so out loud matters, since COM3 on the desk in front of you is not it.
    /// </summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<string> _serialPorts = [];

    /// <summary>
    /// What Connect dials: whichever box is showing. The serial one holds a port name rather
    /// than a whole address — COM3, or COM3?baud=115200 — so the scheme is added here, the
    /// same way and in the same place the desktop adds it, and a pasted serial:// is left
    /// alone. Core reads the result either way (SPEC §5).
    /// </summary>
    private string TypedAddress
    {
        get
        {
            if (!_serial) return _address;

            string chosen = _serialPort.Trim();
            if (chosen.Length == 0) return "";
            return chosen.StartsWith("serial://", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
                ? chosen
                : "serial://" + chosen;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Put the whole card into one mode or the other.
    ///
    /// The list is emptied on the way through. Rows from the other mode are not merely stale,
    /// they are unreadable — an IP address under a column headed "Port" — and a row that
    /// fills the Address box has to fill it with something the visible box can hold.
    ///
    /// Switching to Serial lists the server's ports straight away, unasked and unopened.
    /// That is already enough to pick one and connect; Scan is what opens them.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task SetScanKind(bool serial)
    {
        if (_serial == serial) return;

        _serial = serial;
        _devices.Clear();
        _hosts = 0;
        _probed = 0;

        if (!serial)
        {
            _scanStatus = "Ready.";
            return;
        }

        await ListSerialPortsAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The server's ports as rows, unasked. The counterpart of the desktop's list filling in
    /// the moment the card switches — opens nothing, and says how to get some when there are
    /// none.
    ///
    /// Rows from the server rather than names turned into rows here, so the line settings
    /// shown are the ones Core would actually use rather than a string that agreed with
    /// SerialSettings.Default on the day it was typed.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task ListSerialPortsAsync()
    {
        _devices.Clear();

        try
        {
            foreach (DeviceDto row in await Bench.SerialPortRowsAsync()) _devices.Add(row);
        }
        catch
        {
            // The server is unreachable, which the rest of the page is already saying.
        }

        _serialPorts = _devices.Select(d => d.Address).ToList();

        _scanStatus = _devices.Count == 0
            ? "No serial ports on the server."
            : $"{_devices.Count} port(s) listed. Scan asks each one for its identity.";
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Re-read the server's ports. On focus rather than once at startup, for the desktop's
    /// reason: an adapter appears the moment it is plugged in, and the moment someone looks
    /// at this list is exactly when they have just plugged one in. Listing never opens a
    /// port, so there is nothing to be careful about in calling it often.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task LoadPortsAsync()
    {
        try { _serialPorts = await Bench.PortsAsync(); }
        catch { _serialPorts = []; }

        // One port and nothing typed: choose it, as the desktop does. An empty box in front
        // of a list of one reads as a list of none.
        if (_serialPort.Length == 0 && _serialPorts.Count == 1) _serialPort = _serialPorts[0];
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// How long to wait for an instrument, on the connection and on every query afterwards.
    /// The desktop's spinner starts at 3000 and takes 100 to 30000 in steps of 500.
    /// </summary>
    private int _timeoutMs = 3000;

    /// <summary>The connection being made, so Connect can double as Cancel.</summary>
    private CancellationTokenSource? _connect;
    /// <summary>What the line beside the bar says. "Ready." before anything has been asked
    /// of it, as the desktop's status label starts.</summary>
    private string _scanStatus = "Ready.";
    private string? _connectError;
    private bool _scanning;
    private bool _connecting;

    private int _hosts;
    private int _probed;
    private string _detail = "";
    private CancellationTokenSource? _scan;

    private int Percent => _hosts <= 0 ? 0 : Math.Min(100, (int)(100L * _probed / _hosts));

    /// <summary>The address the browser refused to open, and the name it was to open under.</summary>
    private string? _blocked;
    private string _blockedName = "";

    /// <summary>The tab on top: a session id, or null for the bench itself.</summary>
    private string? _selected;

    /// <summary>Sessions whose console is open in a window of its own.</summary>
    private readonly HashSet<string> _out = [];

    private DotNetObjectReference<Instruments>? _self;

    /// <summary>
    /// The bench changed — here or in another window. Re-read the list rather than patching it:
    /// what is on the bench is a question with an answer already, and a page that guesses at the
    /// change would be keeping a second copy of it.
    /// </summary>
    private async void OnBenchChanged()
    {
        _sessions = await Bench.SessionsAsync();
        SettleSelection();
        await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
    }

    private bool IsOut(string id) => _out.Contains(id);

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        _interfaces = await Bench.InterfacesAsync();
        _interfaceAddress = _interfaces.FirstOrDefault(i => i.HasGateway)?.Address ?? _interfaces.FirstOrDefault()?.Address;
        InterfaceChanged();
        _sessions = await Bench.SessionsAsync();

        if (SessionFromQuery is not null && _sessions.Any(s => s.Id == SessionFromQuery))
            _selected = SessionFromQuery;

        SettleSelection();

        // And stay told. The bench is one shared workspace: something connected in another
        // window, or gone from one, changes what belongs in this tab strip — and a page that
        // only ever read the list when it did something itself keeps drawing a console onto a
        // session the server no longer has. The queue and the lock were already pushed for the
        // same reason; the list itself was the one that was not.
        //
        // Through BenchClient's event rather than off the hub directly, so a change made here
        // and a change made in another window arrive by the same road — and so the status bar,
        // which counts the same list, cannot end up disagreeing with this strip.
        Bench.SessionsChanged += OnBenchChanged;
        await Bench.HubAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Keep the current tab on a console that exists. With the tabs down in their own group
    /// there is no Bench tab to fall back to and no "no tab" state to be in: while anything is
    /// connected one of them is current, the way a TabControl always has a selected page.
    /// </summary>
    private void SettleSelection()
    {
        if (_selected is not null && _sessions.Any(s => s.Id == _selected)) return;
        _selected = _sessions.FirstOrDefault()?.Id;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Ask which consoles are already in windows of their own, and stay listening for the
    /// ones that open and close from now on.
    ///
    /// Asked, not remembered: a list kept in storage goes stale the moment a detached window
    /// is closed while this page is shut, and a stale entry would hide a console behind a
    /// window that is not there. Presence is proved by an answer.
    /// </summary>
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool first)
    {
        if (!first) return;
        _self = DotNetObjectReference.Create(this);
        await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("lec.windows.listen", _self);
    }

    /// <summary>A window has just announced itself. Dim its tab now rather than waiting for
    /// the next roll-call.</summary>
    [JSInvokable]
    public Task Detached(string id)
        => _out.Add(id) ? InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged) : Task.CompletedTask;

    /// <summary>A window said it was closing. A courtesy — <see cref="Present"/> is what is
    /// actually believed.</summary>
    [JSInvokable]
    public Task Attached(string id)
        => _out.Remove(id) ? InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged) : Task.CompletedTask;

    /// <summary>
    /// The answer to the roll-call, for the windows that are also routes: every one genuinely in
    /// a browser tab of its own, right now. Whatever was sent out and did not answer has had its
    /// tab closed, and comes back here as a window — which is where it was before "Open in a tab"
    /// moved it.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The round trip a detached console already makes. Without it, moving one into a tab and
    /// closing that tab is a window that has simply gone, and getting it back means remembering
    /// which menu it came from.
    ///
    /// A roll-call rather than a message from the tab as it closes, for the reason
    /// <see cref="Present"/> gives: that message does not survive the document being torn down.
    /// </remarks>
    [JSInvokable]
    public Task ToolsPresent(string[] routes)
        => _windows?.SettleAsync(routes) ?? Task.CompletedTask;

    /// <summary>
    /// A tab spoke up for one of those windows. Only a window that has been heard from can be
    /// noticed going quiet — see <see cref="WindowDialog"/>.
    /// </summary>
    [JSInvokable]
    public void ToolSeen(string route) => _windows?.Heard(route);

    /// <summary>
    /// The answer to a roll-call: every console that is genuinely in a window of its own,
    /// right now. This replaces what was believed rather than adding to it, which is the
    /// point — it is the only thing that can clear an entry for a window that died without
    /// getting the chance to say so.
    /// </summary>
    [JSInvokable]
    public Task Present(string[] ids)
    {
        if (_out.SetEquals(ids)) return Task.CompletedTask;
        _out.Clear();
        foreach (string id in ids) _out.Add(id);
        return InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
    }

    private void Show(string id) => _selected = id;

    // ------------------------------------------------------------- the tab menu

    /// <summary>Which tab was right-clicked, and where the pointer was.</summary>
    private string? _tabMenu;
    private double _menuX, _menuY;

    /// <summary>Whether the button went down on the sheet outside the menu rather than in it —
    /// the same rule as everywhere else here: a drag that overshoots does not dismiss.</summary>
    private bool _downOutside;

    private void OpenTabMenu(string id, MouseEventArgs e)
    {
        _tabMenu = id;
        _menuX = e.ClientX;
        _menuY = e.ClientY;
        _downOutside = false;
    }

    private void CloseTabMenu()
    {
        _tabMenu = null;
        _downOutside = false;
    }

    private void ReleasedOutside()
    {
        if (_downOutside) CloseTabMenu();
        _downOutside = false;
    }

    private async Task DisconnectFromMenu()
    {
        string? id = _tabMenu;
        CloseTabMenu();
        if (id is not null) await Disconnect(id);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The row as an address to type back in. Worked out by Core and carried on the row —
    /// this used to read <c>d.Port == 111 ? …</c>, which is Core's rule spelled a second time
    /// here and nowhere in the desktop, so the two builds showed different things for one
    /// instrument. The fallback covers a row from an older server.
    /// </summary>
    private static string Endpoint(DeviceDto d) =>
        string.IsNullOrEmpty(d.TypedAddress) ? $"{d.Address}:{d.Port}" : d.TypedAddress;

    /// <summary>
    /// Bring a detached console's window to the front without touching what is in it.
    /// Detaching is a link, because it is an address; this is not, because reloading a
    /// console that has been running for an hour is not what "show me it" means.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task Raise(string id)
    {
        string url = $"console?session={id}&detached=1";
        _blockedName = $"lec-console-{id}";
        bool ok = await JS.InvokeAsync<bool>("lec.windows.raise", url, _blockedName);
        _blocked = ok ? null : url;
    }

    /// <summary>Ask a detached console to come home. It closes itself, which is what tells
    /// this page to un-dim the tab — the same route as any other window closing.</summary>
    private async Task Recall(string id) => await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("lec.windows.recall", id);

    /// <summary>
    /// Sweep, and draw what comes back as it comes back.
    ///
    /// A /24 takes a while and a /20 takes minutes, and a page that shows nothing at all
    /// until the end is indistinguishable from one that has hung. So the server reports as it
    /// goes: how far along it is, and every instrument the moment it answers.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task Scan()
    {
        _scan?.Cancel();
        _scan = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _scanning = true;
        _scanStatus = "Starting…";
        _detail = "";
        _hosts = 0;
        _probed = 0;
        _devices.Clear();

        // Serial keeps every port in the list whether or not it answers — the rows are already
        // there from switching to this mode, and Scan fills identities into them rather than
        // building a list from nothing. So the two sweeps clear different things: a subnet
        // sweep starts from an empty list, and this one starts from the ports.
        if (_serial) await ListSerialPortsAsync();

        try
        {
            var stream = _serial
                ? Bench.ScanSerialStreamAsync(new SerialScanRequest(_serialScope, _bauds), _scan.Token)
                : Bench.ScanStreamAsync(new ScanRequest(_interfaceAddress, _range, _ports), _scan.Token);

            await foreach (var e in stream)
            {
                switch (e.Stage)
                {
                    case ScanStage.Started:
                        _hosts = e.Hosts;
                        _detail = e.Detail ?? "";
                        _scanStatus = $"Scanning {_detail}…";
                        break;

                    case ScanStage.Probed:
                        _hosts = e.Hosts;
                        // The scanner reports from several tasks at once, so a report can
                        // arrive behind one that overtook it. A bar that walks backwards
                        // reads as a fault, and the highest figure seen is the true one.
                        _probed = Math.Max(_probed, e.Probed);
                        _scanStatus = $"Scanning {_detail}… {_probed:N0}/{_hosts:N0} probed, {Answered}.";
                        break;

                    case ScanStage.Found:
                        if (e.Device is not null) Found(e.Device);
                        break;

                    case ScanStage.Done:
                        _probed = _hosts;
                        _scanStatus = e.Error ?? (_serial
                            ? $"Scan complete. {Answered} of {e.Hosts} port(s)."
                            : $"Scan complete. {Answered} across {e.Hosts:N0} address(es).");
                        if (e.Error is not null) _scanStatus += $" {Answered} so far.";
                        break;
                }
                StateHasChanged();
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // The Stop button, or this page going away. Whatever answered before the stop is
            // already listed, and stays listed — a partial sweep is still a result.
            _scanStatus = $"Scan stopped after {_probed:N0} of {_hosts:N0} address(es). {Answered}.";
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            _scanStatus = "Scan failed: " + ex.Message;
        }
        finally
        {
            _scanning = false;
            _scan?.Dispose();
            _scan = null;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// A row arrived. A network sweep only ever reports things that answered, so the row is
    /// new; a serial sweep reports every port it asked, and that port is already listed —
    /// appending would show COM3 twice, once as found and once as asked.
    /// </summary>
    private void Found(DeviceDto row)
    {
        int at = _devices.FindIndex(d => string.Equals(d.Address, row.Address, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase));
        if (_serial && at >= 0) _devices[at] = row;
        else _devices.Add(row);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// What the status line counts. Every serial port is a row whether or not anything is on
    /// it, so counting rows there would report a machine with four unused adapters as four
    /// instruments; what counts is the ones that answered.
    /// </summary>
    private string Answered
    {
        get
        {
            int n = _devices.Count(d => d.Answered);
            return n == 1 ? "1 instrument found" : $"{n} instruments found";
        }
    }

    private void Stop() => _scan?.Cancel();

    /// <summary>
    /// A row was clicked. The desktop fills the Address box with the full endpoint — including
    /// the port the scan actually found, which is the point of doing it at all: a Rigol that
    /// answered on 5555 is not the same address as one assumed on 5025.
    /// </summary>
    private void Pick(DeviceDto d)
    {
        if (!_serial)
        {
            _address = Endpoint(d);
            return;
        }

        // A serial row fills the port box, so it goes in without the scheme: that box is a
        // list of serial ports and should read as one. The line settings come with it when
        // they are not the default, because they are the ones that answered — dropping them
        // would connect at 9600 to an instrument the scan found at 115200.
        string typed = Endpoint(d);
        _serialPort = typed.StartsWith("serial://", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
            ? typed["serial://".Length..]
            : typed;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Write the discovered list out as CSV, as the desktop's Export Results does. The file is
    /// built on the server by Core's writer, so it is the same file from either build.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task ExportResults()
    {
        if (_devices.Count == 0) return;
        string csv = await Bench.ExportDevicesAsync(_devices);
        string base64 = Convert.ToBase64String(System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(csv));
        // The browser has no Save As dialog to offer, so the name the desktop's dialog opens
        // with is the name the file gets.
        await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("lec.download", "discovered-instruments.csv", "text/csv", base64);
    }

    private async Task Connect(string address)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(address)) return;
        _connecting = true;
        _connectError = null;
        _connect = new CancellationTokenSource();
        try
        {
            // Clamped to the same range the desktop's spinner allows, because a number box in
            // a browser will hand you whatever was typed into it.
            var (session, error) = await Bench.ConnectAsync(
                address, Math.Clamp(_timeoutMs, 100, 30000), _connect.Token);
            if (session is null) { _connectError = error; return; }

            // Asked before the list is refreshed: the server hands back the session it already
            // has for an address rather than opening a second, so an id that was already on the
            // bench is how "already connected" is told from "connected".
            bool already = _sessions.Any(s => s.Id == session.Id);

            _sessions = await Bench.SessionsAsync();
            // The desktop app selects a new tab as it makes it, and the reason you connected
            // is to talk to the thing.
            _selected = session.Id;
            _connectError = null;

            // And the desktop says so in the console, because bringing a tab to the front that
            // was already at the front looks like the button did nothing at all.
            if (already && ConsoleFor(session.Id) is { } console) await console.SayAlreadyConnectedAsync();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) { _connectError = "Connection cancelled."; }
        finally
        {
            _connecting = false;
            _connect?.Dispose();
            _connect = null;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Give up on the connection. The server drops the socket on its way out, so
    /// nothing is left half-open behind it.</summary>
    private void CancelConnect() => _connect?.Cancel();

    /// <summary>
    /// Close a console and drop its connection.
    ///
    /// The server is asked first and the tab goes either way. If the ask fails there is nothing
    /// left to disconnect from — the usual reason is that the server itself has gone, and the
    /// tabs on screen are drawn from a list it answered while it was still there. Leaving the
    /// tab up would mean offering a console onto an instrument nothing is holding.
    ///
    /// It used to be one unguarded await, so a server that went away between the page loading
    /// and the ✕ being pressed threw out of the click handler — and an unhandled exception in a
    /// component takes the whole page down with "Something went wrong. Reload the page." Closing
    /// a tab must never be able to do that, least of all when the reason it failed is that the
    /// thing you were connected to is already gone.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task Disconnect(string id)
    {
        try
        {
            await Bench.DisconnectAsync(id);
            _sessions = await Bench.SessionsAsync();
            _connectError = null;
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            _sessions = _sessions.Where(s => s.Id != id).ToList();
            _connectError = "Closed the tab, but the server did not answer: " + ex.Message;
        }

        _out.Remove(id);
        // Closing the current tab moves to whatever is left, as closing a tab does anywhere.
        SettleSelection();
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        // A sweep nobody is watching is a sweep the bench network can do without. Cancelling
        // here reaches the server: the stream ends, and the token it is running under trips.
        _scan?.Cancel();
        Bench.SessionsChanged -= OnBenchChanged;

        try { await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("lec.windows.forget"); }
        catch (JSDisconnectedException) { /* the page is going away; so is the listener */ }
        _self?.Dispose();
    }
}
